WEBVTT

00:05.000 --> 00:13.220
Stomachs are full of hydrochloric acid. This acid helps break down food and kill bacteria.

00:15.080 --> 00:19.840
Thankfully, stomachs have a strong lining that prevents the acid from causing pain.

00:22.880 --> 00:29.400
The lower esophageal sphincter acts as a door between the esophagus and stomach. It allows

00:29.400 --> 00:38.000
food in and does its best to keep stomach acid from escaping. We experience discomfort when

00:38.000 --> 00:43.100
stomach acid does escape through the lower esophageal sphincter into the esophagus, which,

00:43.340 --> 00:49.820
unlike the stomach, has no protective lining. When this happens, the acid burns the esophagus.

00:50.760 --> 00:54.960
This is heartburn, and it can be quite painful.

01:06.040 --> 01:16.000
There are three main groups of drugs that treat heartburn. Antacids, H2 antagonists, and proton

01:16.000 --> 01:20.520
pump inhibitors. Each fight heartburn differently.

01:22.720 --> 01:29.820
An antacid is a weak base. When a base comes into contact with an acid, the acid is neutralised

01:29.820 --> 01:37.920
and converted into salt and water. As the antacid moves down the esophagus, it neutralises escaped

01:37.920 --> 01:44.360
acid along the way. Once in the stomach, the antacid lowers the overall acidity of the stomach.

01:45.620 --> 01:51.020
Antacids offer immediate, short-term relief by combating the acid head-on.

01:53.200 --> 02:00.780
Now, on to H2 antagonists. The stomach is lined with parietal cells that create stomach acid.

02:01.040 --> 02:10.060
But the parietal cells only make acid when they are told to do so. Acetylcholine, gastrin, and histamine

02:10.060 --> 02:16.560
are the three chemicals that land on specific binding sites on the parietal cell, and in

02:16.560 --> 02:19.000
doing so, tell it to produce acid.

02:21.460 --> 02:29.300
An H2 antagonist blocks the binding site for histamine. When this happens, histamine cannot bind to the

02:29.300 --> 02:36.580
parietal cell, and thus cannot signal acid production. The parietal cell therefore only receives two

02:36.580 --> 02:42.320
out of three commands to produce acid. This means less stomach acidity and discomfort.

02:43.460 --> 02:51.740
H2 antagonists start working in about 30 minutes, and can last up to 12 hours. If they are taken day

02:51.740 --> 02:59.040
after day, however, they become less and less effective. Because of this, H2 antagonists may

02:59.040 --> 03:09.480
not be the best solution for frequent heartburn. Proton pump inhibitors, or PPIs, target a different

03:09.480 --> 03:17.820
part of the parietal cell. Every day, parietal cells are producing numerous proton pumps. The proton

03:17.820 --> 03:25.820
pump transports acid from the parietal cell into the stomach. A PPI works by blocking these pumps and

03:25.820 --> 03:34.080
stopping acid secretion. Once the PPI binds to a pump, it turns it off for good. But there will be new

03:34.080 --> 03:41.400
proton pumps tomorrow, so a PPI must be taken daily. Though PPIs are the slowest of the three drugs to

03:41.400 --> 03:46.640
take effect, they are the best solution for relief from frequent heartburn.

